## Theme 2: Platform Strategy & Multi-Party Prioritization

Managing a frontier model is a platform challenge: you are building an engine that powers an entire ecosystem of distinct vehicles.

### Key insights from the conversation
- Capabilities Over Features: When balancing requests from first-party tools, second-party surfaces, and third-party enterprise cloud customers, you cannot simply stack feature requests without understanding the underlying model capability.
- Symbiotic Feedback Flywheels: Treating core internal surfaces as “hero partners” creates a high-velocity feedback loop for debugging model losses and testing frontier research.
- Agility vs. Stability: Internal surfaces allow rapid iteration and experimentation, while external API tiers require package stability so customer production environments are not disrupted.

### Practical learnings & actionable takeaways
- Abstract the Request: When a stakeholder asks for a specific feature, abstract it to the core model requirement. For example, instead of “add an auto-summary button to Gmail,” evaluate the underlying capability needed.
- Dogfood Internally First: Use first-party product surfaces as a proving ground to build conviction and iron out model flaws before locking down API capabilities for enterprise customers.
- Focus ROI at the Capability Level: Evaluate roadmap priority by measuring how many downstream surfaces and business verticals a single model capability enhancement can unlock.

