## Theme 3: Talent Architecture & Interdisciplinary DNA

The traditional mold of the hyper-specialized computer science PM has evolved into something fluid, curious, and data-driven.

### Key insights from the conversation
- Diversity of Backgrounds: Successful model teams blend engineering talent with PMs from finance, operations, consulting, and design.
- Data Curiosity Over Deep Research: PMs do not need to write model code or be researchers, but they do need significant intellectual curiosity—drilling two or three layers below top-line metrics to understand the real story.
- Calibrating to Researcher Mental Models: Earning researcher trust requires high emotional intelligence. Some researchers are customer-motivated; others are vision-driven and need a PM to help translate long-term strategy into execution.

### Practical learnings & actionable takeaways
- Drill Below Surface Metrics: Never accept top-line accuracy at face value (for example, “95% accuracy”). Manually inspect failure cases, parse log distributions, and understand why the remaining 5% fail.
- Adapt Your PM Style to Your Tech Lead: Assess whether your research or engineering partners are end-user focused or technology-focused, and tailor how you synthesize and operationalize feedback.
- Leverage Modern Tooling (“PM Twins”): Use AI tools internally for repetitive tasks—such as feedback categorization, data analysis, and slide generation—to free up bandwidth for more strategic thinking.

